%% md
# 01 — Logistic regression on real clinical data: who develops diabetes?

**Dataset.** Pima Indians Diabetes (NIDDK, 1988): 768 women aged 21+, 8 measurements taken at a check-up, and whether
they were diagnosed with diabetes within 5 years. Small, messy, and a classic — which is exactly why it is good
practice: it has a trap that most online tutorials fall into.

**What you will learn — as an engineer, not just as a student of the formula**
- Look at the data *before* any model, and find what is wrong with it.
- Split first, then preprocess, so nothing from the test set leaks into training.
- Always beat a dumb baseline before celebrating.
- Write logistic regression yourself (sigmoid, loss, gradient, training loop), then check it against scikit-learn.
- Discover *by experiment* why feature scaling matters for gradient descent.
- Read coefficients as odds ratios — and know when not to trust them.
- Turn probabilities into decisions: thresholds, sensitivity, PPV.

**Time:** ~2–2.5 h. You can stop after any part.

**Legend**

| | |
|---|---|
| 📖 | read — the idea, short |
| 🔮 | predict before you run — write a guess and a confidence tag `[1]` guessing / `[2]` fairly sure / `[3]` certain |
| ✍️ | your code — usually 1–5 lines, `???` marks what to write |
| 🧪 | explore — open-ended, no check, no right answer; write one line about what you saw |
| 🛠️ | engineer's habit — something you will do on every project from now on |

Hints are folded in `▶ Hint` blocks. Open them after ~10 minutes stuck, not before.

%% code
import numpy as np
import pandas as pd
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
from mlj.datasets import load_pima

rng = np.random.default_rng(0)
pd.set_option("display.precision", 3)
plt.rcParams["figure.figsize"] = (6, 3.5)

def check(cond, msg):
    print(("✅ " if cond else "❌ ") + msg)

%% md
## Part 1 — Meet the data (20 min)

🛠️ **Habit: never fit a model on data you have not looked at.** Five minutes of `describe()` and histograms catch
problems that no amount of tuning will fix.

%% code
df = load_pima()
df.head()

%% code
df.describe().T

%% md
🔮 Look at the `min` column above. Which values are physiologically impossible for a living person?

> Prediction:

%% code
fig, axes = plt.subplots(2, 4, figsize=(13, 5))
for ax, col in zip(axes.flat, df.columns[:-1]):
    ax.hist(df[col], bins=30, color="tab:blue", alpha=0.8)
    ax.set_title(col, fontsize=9)
plt.tight_layout(); plt.show()

%% md
See those spikes at 0? A blood pressure of 0 means a dead patient, not a healthy one. In this dataset **0 was used to
mean "not measured"**. Pregnancies = 0 is fine (it is a real count), the others are not.

✍️ Count the zeros in each column, then replace the impossible zeros by `NaN` in a copy of the dataframe.

%% code
impossible_zero = ["Glucose", "BloodPressure", "SkinThickness", "Insulin", "BMI"]

#<<SOL
zeros = (df == 0).sum()
print(zeros)
df_clean = df.copy()
df_clean[impossible_zero] = df_clean[impossible_zero].replace(0, np.nan)
#==
zeros = ???          # number of zeros per column (one line, no loop)
print(zeros)
df_clean = df.copy()
???                  # zeros -> NaN, only in the impossible_zero columns
#>>

print("\nfraction missing:\n", df_clean.isna().mean().round(3))
check(df_clean["Insulin"].isna().sum() == 374 and df_clean["Pregnancies"].isna().sum() == 0, "zeros replaced in the right columns")

%% md
Almost half of `Insulin` is missing. A tutorial that leaves the zeros in is telling the model that "insulin = 0" is a
real low value. It is not — it is "the doctor did not order the test".

🧪 **Is missingness informative?** Compare the diabetes rate for patients whose insulin was measured vs not. If they
differ, the *fact of being measured* carries information (doctors order tests when they suspect something). This
matters a lot in clinical data, and has a name: *informative missingness*.

%% code
#<<SOL
print(df_clean.groupby(df_clean["Insulin"].isna())["Outcome"].agg(["mean", "size"]))
#==
# one groupby: Outcome rate when Insulin is missing vs present
#>>

%% md
> What I see:

📖 **Prevalence** = fraction of positives. Here about 35 %. Write it down — every number later must be read against
it. (In the real population it is much lower: this cohort was selected for being at high risk.)

%% md
## Part 2 — Split first, preprocess second (10 min)

🛠️ **Habit: split before anything that learns from data — including imputation and scaling.** If you compute the
median of `Insulin` on the full dataset, a bit of the test set leaks into training. With 768 rows it barely matters;
on a real project it is the classic way to get a result nobody can reproduce.

We use 60 % train, 20 % validation (to make choices), 20 % test (looked at **once**, at the very end). `stratify`
keeps the prevalence identical in the three parts.

%% code
from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split

features = [c for c in df_clean.columns if c != "Outcome"]
trainval, test = train_test_split(df_clean, test_size=0.2, stratify=df_clean["Outcome"], random_state=0)
train, val = train_test_split(trainval, test_size=0.25, stratify=trainval["Outcome"], random_state=0)
print(len(train), len(val), len(test), "| prevalence:", [round(d["Outcome"].mean(), 3) for d in (train, val, test)])

%% md
✍️ Impute the missing values with the **training** medians, and apply the same medians to val and test.

%% code
#<<SOL
medians = train[features].median()
def impute(d):
    return d[features].fillna(medians)
#==
medians = ???                       # computed on train only
def impute(d):
    return ???                      # returns d[features] with NaN replaced by the train medians
#>>

X_tr, X_va, X_te = impute(train), impute(val), impute(test)
y_tr, y_va, y_te = train["Outcome"].to_numpy(), val["Outcome"].to_numpy(), test["Outcome"].to_numpy()
check(not X_tr.isna().any().any() and not X_te.isna().any().any(), "no NaN left")
check(np.isclose(medians["Insulin"], train["Insulin"].median()), "medians come from train")

